## What SOLID Stands for in Software Design

**SOLID** is an acronym for five foundational design principles in object-oriented software engineering, originally introduced by Robert C. Martin in his 2000 essay *Design Principles and Design Patterns* and later named by Michael Feathers. [6](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [10](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) The principles are intended to make software more understandable, flexible, scalable, and maintainable. [6](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [2](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)

The five letters stand for:

- **S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should take on just one responsibility and handle it well. [1](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [4](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)
- **O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification, allowing new behavior to be added without altering existing code. [4](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [12](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)
- **L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):** Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without breaking the correctness of the program. [2](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [4](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)
- **I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):** Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use; prefer smaller, specific interfaces over large, general-purpose ones. [4](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [12](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)
- **D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; instead, both should depend on abstractions. [4](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [5](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)

By following SOLID, developers reduce coupling between classes, making codebases easier to test, extend, and refactor as projects grow. [8](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi) [2](#sTbEEaPdsMME6kBi)

