### Key Market Dynamics

The 2026 landscape is defined by a sharp transatlantic divide. Europe's aggressive subsidy programs—most notably Germany's **€3 billion initiative**—have supercharged demand, attracting both domestic and foreign manufacturers. [1](#64591a58-1a87-42e3-ac86-1ccc1a0528c0) Paradoxically, Tesla and BYD emerged as the two largest beneficiaries of German subsidies, raising questions about whether taxpayer funds are strengthening international competitors over domestic brands. [1](#64591a58-1a87-42e3-ac86-1ccc1a0528c0)

In contrast, the U.S. market continues to adjust to the post-tax-credit reality. After a record **437,487 EV sales** in Q4 2025 as buyers rushed to claim the expiring incentive, sales plunged 46% in the following quarter and bottomed out in Q1 2026. [4](#3fdc05f1-e7a5-48e8-a46f-15b6638076df) The Q2 rebound suggests the market may be stabilizing, supported by state-level incentives and new affordable model launches. [4](#3fdc05f1-e7a5-48e8-a46f-15b6638076df)

### Comparison to 2025

For context, full-year 2025 was a record-breaking year with global EV sales reaching approximately **20.7 to 21.6 million units** depending on the tracker. [5](#a1a6a7b6-5019-472f-bb88-df0e6114bbf0) China accounted for roughly 60% of global sales, while Europe delivered around 4.3 million units. If H1 2026's trajectory holds, 2026 is on track to set another annual record, though growth is heavily concentrated in Europe and developing markets while China and North America face headwinds.

