## Caddy Is the Least Painful for Automatic HTTPS

**Caddy** is the clear winner if your goal is the fastest, most hands-off setup for automatic HTTPS. [1](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2)

### Why Caddy Wins

- **Zero-configuration TLS:** With a simple `Caddyfile`, you get automatic TLS, redirects, and reverse proxying without fiddling with extra tooling. [2](#3be3e8ee-2aa3-40a7-8bb0-4f1f9c9d1b75) In many cases, simply providing a hostname is enough for Caddy to obtain and renew certificates from Let's Encrypt or ZeroSSL. [3](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2)
- **No external tooling:** Unlike nginx, Caddy does not require a separate ACME client such as Certbot; certificate lifecycle management is handled inside the single server binary. [4](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2)
- **Built-in modern protocols:** Caddy provides built-in support for HTTP/3 and other modern protocols, reducing the need for manual feature toggles. [5](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2)

### How Traefik Compares

Traefik supports automatic HTTPS natively, but it is more involved. It demands explicit configuration of certificate resolvers, challenge types, and domain policies before it will issue or renew certificates. [6](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2) [7](#3be3e8ee-2aa3-40a7-8bb0-4f1f9c9d1b75) Traefik also asks you to learn its vocabulary—routers, services, entrypoints, providers, and middlewares—before the setup feels elegant. [8](#3be3e8ee-2aa3-40a7-8bb0-4f1f9c9d1b75)

### How nginx Compares

Nginx is the most manual option. It has no native certificate provisioning and relies on external tools like Certbot, requiring manual configuration of certificate paths and renewal schedules. [9](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2) This creates multiple moving parts instead of one and leaves more room for human error or expired certificates. [10](#f3ec595a-6d86-4ba9-85f1-794913ddfca2)

### Bottom Line

If your priority is the least painful path to automatic HTTPS, **Caddy** is the best choice: it treats secure TLS as a default behavior rather than an add-on. Traefik provides automation but requires upfront setup, while nginx remains a largely manual process dependent on external tools.

