## What is systemd-resolved and why people disable it

### What is systemd-resolved?

systemd-resolved is a systemd component that handles DNS name lookups for the system. [2](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU) On most installations it manages `/etc/resolv.conf` via a symlink, which is why the file commonly points to the local stub address `127.0.0.53`. [6](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU) It also supports advanced security features such as DNSSEC and DNS-over-TLS. [2](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU)

### Reasons people disable it

- **Running a custom DNS server.** Administrators who set up their own resolver—such as Unbound or a dedicated server for a WireGuard setup—frequently disable systemd-resolved to avoid having two conflicting resolvers on the same system. [2](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU) [8](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU)

- **Direct control over `/etc/resolv.conf`.** Many administrators prefer to manage DNS servers manually and find that systemd-resolved repeatedly alters or overwrites `/etc/resolv.conf`, so they disable it to use the file directly. [4](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU) [7](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU) [9](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU)

- **Added complexity.** Some system administrators feel that systemd-resolved adds an unnecessary abstraction layer that complicates tasks previously handled by simple config files and traditional resolver settings. [8](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU)

One practical caution is that outright disabling the service can also remove the mechanism that applies DHCP-provided DNS settings, so an alternative DNS configuration plan is usually needed. [8](#HYN7e5PZps9DO6YU)

